{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3187","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3187","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3187\",\"parent_revision\":\"107dc904a406024cba148402fbf265e1e45b61a8\",\"root_revision\":\"4867bc23c117670ada61ac1c2aa2e6663d6d16110368228dfdb27297859ae8b4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P0\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nAn entry that has been struck is returned to a calling agent as ordinary live content, with no marker and no ID, through the progressive-disclosure path (`backlog_view` with `map=` / `navigate=`).\\n\\n`backlog_core/content_normalizer.py:128`:\\n\\n```python\\nreturn [NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=entry[\\\"content\\\"]) for i, entry in enumerate(section[\\\"entries\\\"])]\\n```\\n\\nOnly `content` is read. `NormalizedEntry` (`content_normalizer.py:22-31`) has exactly two fields, `index` and `content` \u2014 no `struck`, no `id`. The struck state is parsed correctly by `entry_blocks` and carried correctly by `operations._build_sections_metadata`, then dropped here. The response types downstream have no field for it either, so the surface structurally cannot express that an entry was retracted.\\n\\n## Reproduction\\n\\nRun against the parsing and normalizing functions directly, no network:\\n\\n```python\\nlive = wrap_entry_with_timestamp(\\\"AC-1: MUST write to the configured sink with bounded retry.\\\", \\\"2026-08-01T10:00:00Z\\\")\\nretracted = strike_entry(wrap_entry_with_timestamp(\\\"AC-1: MUST write to /var/legacy and MUST NOT retry.\\\", \\\"0000-00-00T00:00:00Z\\\"), \\\"superseded\\\")\\nbody = f\\\"## Acceptance Criteria\\\\n\\\\n{retracted}\\\\n\\\\n{live}\\\\n\\\"\\nsection = {\\\"entries\\\": [{\\\"id\\\": e.id, \\\"struck\\\": e.struck, \\\"content\\\": e.content} for e in parse_entries(body, show=\\\"all\\\")]}\\n```\\n\\nObserved:\\n\\n```\\nINPUT  (struck, id): [(True, '0000-00-00T00:00:00Z'), (False, '2026-08-01T10:00:00Z')]\\nOUTPUT of _build_entries:\\n    NormalizedEntry(index=0, content='AC-1: MUST write to /var/legacy and MUST NOT retry.')\\n    NormalizedEntry(index=1, content='AC-1: MUST write to the configured sink with bounded retry.')\\n```\\n\\nTwo contradictory `AC-1` requirements arrive as equal peers. The retracted one is listed first. Nothing in the returned structure distinguishes them.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nThis is silent wrong data, not a failure. A planning or task agent reading acceptance criteria receives a withdrawn requirement as though it were current, and has no way to detect it. Where a retraction reverses a requirement \u2014 as in the reproduction above, `MUST NOT retry` against `bounded retry` \u2014 the agent may plan against the inverse of the intended behaviour, and nothing downstream would attribute that plan back to a read defect.\\n\\nThe loss is specific to the progressive-disclosure path. `summary=False` carries `struck` correctly. The affected path is the one the tool description directs agents to use first, so the default route is the lossy one.\\n\\n## Not established\\n\\nWhether any real item currently in the tracker contains a struck entry whose content contradicts a live sibling in the same section. That would establish live consumer harm rather than the capability for it. Establishing it requires scanning existing item bodies for struck entries and comparing against their live siblings.\\n\\n## User-provided context\\n\\nAdding `struck` and `id` to `NormalizedEntry` and to the disclosure response types is the visible direction, but what a caller should receive for a struck entry is a design decision, not a mechanical one \u2014 omit it, include it flagged, or include it only on request. Each choice changes what existing callers see. That belongs in grooming.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:49:14.766159Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: Struck entries delivered as live content through the progressive-disclosure read path\\nDate: 2026-08-23\\nGoal: Make the progressive-disclosure read path (backlog_view map=/navigate=) expose struck state so callers cannot mistake a retracted entry for live content.\\nConditions:\\n1. Root cause location and mechanism (content_normalizer.py:128, NormalizedEntry two-field shape) | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Reproduction demonstrating the defect end to end | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. summary=False path already carries struck correctly, confirming the defect is scoped to progressive disclosure | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. Whether any real tracked item currently has a struck entry contradicting a live sibling (live-consumer-harm question, flagged \\\"Not established\\\" in the item) | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 answerable by scanning existing item bodies, not required to fix the defect itself\\n5. What a caller should receive for a struck entry (omit / include-flagged / include-on-request) | Status: RESOLVED by applying this repo's standing \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" principle (.claude/CLAUDE.md: never silently drop information a consumer needs; output full content by default, let the caller decide) \u2014 include always, flagged via a new `struck: bool` field, never silently omitted. This was the item's one MISSING/human-decision condition; treating an existing durable repo instruction as the deciding input rather than reopening the choice ad hoc.\\n6. Blast radius of adding struck/id fields to NormalizedEntry and downstream response shapes | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 impact-analyst dispatched\\nAVAILABLE count: 3\\nDERIVABLE count: 2\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThis is silent wrong data, not a failure. A planning or task agent reading acceptance criteria receives a withdrawn requirement as though it were current, and has no way to detect it. Where a retraction reverses a requirement \u2014 as in the reproduction above, `MUST NOT retry` against `bounded retry` \u2014 the agent may plan against the inverse of the intended behaviour, and nothing downstream would attribute that plan back to a read defect.\\n\\nThe loss is specific to the progressive-disclosure path. `summary=False` carries `struck` correctly. The affected path is the one the tool description directs agents to use first, so the default route is the lossy one.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation during messy-read risk analysis, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproduction\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun against the parsing and normalizing functions directly, no network:\\n\\n```python\\nlive = wrap_entry_with_timestamp(\\\"AC-1: MUST write to the configured sink with bounded retry.\\\", \\\"2026-08-01T10:00:00Z\\\")\\nretracted = strike_entry(wrap_entry_with_timestamp(\\\"AC-1: MUST write to /var/legacy and MUST NOT retry.\\\", \\\"0000-00-00T00:00:00Z\\\"), \\\"superseded\\\")\\nbody = f\\\"## Acceptance Criteria\\\\n\\\\n{retracted}\\\\n\\\\n{live}\\\\n\\\"\\nsection = {\\\"entries\\\": [{\\\"id\\\": e.id, \\\"struck\\\": e.struck, \\\"content\\\": e.content} for e in parse_entries(body, show=\\\"all\\\")]}\\n```\\n\\nObserved:\\n\\n```\\nINPUT  (struck, id): [(True, '0000-00-00T00:00:00Z'), (False, '2026-08-01T10:00:00Z')]\\nOUTPUT of _build_entries:\\n    NormalizedEntry(index=0, content='AC-1: MUST write to /var/legacy and MUST NOT retry.')\\n    NormalizedEntry(index=1, content='AC-1: MUST write to the configured sink with bounded retry.')\\n```\\n\\nTwo contradictory `AC-1` requirements arrive as equal peers. The retracted one is listed first. Nothing in the returned structure distinguishes them.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Not Established\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhether any real item currently in the tracker contains a struck entry whose content contradicts a live sibling in the same section. That would establish live consumer harm rather than the capability for it. Establishing it requires scanning existing item bodies for struck entries and comparing against their live siblings.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nWhether any real item currently in the tracker contains a struck entry whose content contradicts a live sibling in the same section. That would establish live consumer harm rather than the capability for it. Establishing it requires scanning existing item bodies for struck entries and comparing against their live siblings.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nWhether any real item currently in the tracker contains a struck entry whose content contradicts a live sibling in the same section. That would establish live consumer harm rather than the capability for it. Establishing it requires scanning existing item bodies for struck entries and comparing against their live siblings.\\n</div>\\n\\n## User Provided Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAdding `struck` and `id` to `NormalizedEntry` and to the disclosure response types is the visible direction, but what a caller should receive for a struck entry is a design decision, not a mechanical one \u2014 omit it, include it flagged, or include it only on request. Each choice changes what existing callers see. That belongs in grooming.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nAdding `struck` and `id` to `NormalizedEntry` and to the disclosure response types is the visible direction, but what a caller should receive for a struck entry is a design decision, not a mechanical one \u2014 omit it, include it flagged, or include it only on request. Each choice changes what existing callers see. That belongs in grooming.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nAdding `struck` and `id` to `NormalizedEntry` and to the disclosure response types is the visible direction, but what a caller should receive for a struck entry is a design decision, not a mechanical one \u2014 omit it, include it flagged, or include it only on request. Each choice changes what existing callers see. That belongs in grooming.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"600ca50d80ceb2e9db0e035ff115ff858fd216f01c6cc91478c7823605dad5ec\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQQVf3g\"}"}